  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct choices are <b>Send an alert to the team if the latency of an Elastic Load Balancer surpasses 10 seconds for a sustained period of two minutes.</b> and <b>Set a CloudWatch alarm to inform the team if the DynamoDB table used for tracking user subscriptions experiences a high rate of throttling.</b><br><br>Both scenarios involve native AWS resources (ELB and DynamoDB), and CloudWatch provides built-in metrics like ELB latency and DynamoDB throttling events, making it easy to configure alarms without custom metrics. These are standard uses for CloudWatch alarms.<br><br><i>Set up notifications when a developer comments on a commit or pull request in AWS CodeCommit.</i> is not suitable as CodeCommit repository activities such as comments are not captured through CloudWatch metrics. Instead, you would use CodeCommit triggers or EventBridge rules.<br><br><i>Automatically launch an additional EC2 instance if average memory usage on an instance exceeds 95% over a five-minute period.</i> is generally not feasible using only default CloudWatch metrics since EC2 memory usage is not enabled by default; it requires the CloudWatch agent for the metric. Additionally, CloudWatch Alarms do not themselves launch instances; this requires further automation such as through Auto Scaling or Lambda functions.<br><br><i>Send an SMS directly to the on-call engineer when critical Tomcat errors are detected on EC2 instances.</i> is not directly achievable with CloudWatch out-of-the-box, unless you are sending Tomcat logs or custom metrics into CloudWatch. Even then, detecting specific Tomcat errors would involve custom log processing or Lambda functions.<br><br><b>TIP:</b> CloudWatch alarms work seamlessly with AWS services that publish standard metrics to CloudWatch. Whenever you need alerting based on resource performance or health (like ELB latency or DynamoDB throttling), CloudWatch Alarms are highly suitable. For custom application or OS-level metrics, additional setup is required.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are working as a DevOps engineer at a financial services organization. To fulfill the requirements of a compliance audit, your task is to implement CloudWatch alarms for various production AWS resources like EC2, DynamoDB, and Lambda. To expedite this, you decide to configure CloudWatch alarms using metrics already provided by CloudWatch. <br><br>Which TWO situations are appropriate to address using CloudWatch alarms based on available CloudWatch metrics?</p>",
